  </div>

  <h3>Five Critical Takeaways</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Business Analysts and Data Analysts face existential risk.</strong> With average AI risk scores of 0.75 and 0.65 respectively, these roles are the most vulnerable to automation through 2035. Their survival scores are the lowest across all job titles.</li>
    <li><strong>AI Researcher and Cybersecurity roles are the most resilient.</strong> These positions combine the lowest automation risk (0.15 and 0.25) with the strongest survival classifications, making them the safest bets for long-term workforce investment.</li>
    <li><strong>Salary growth masks a growing risk premium gap.</strong> Median salaries have grown 52% since 2015, but high-risk roles earn 38% less than medium-risk roles ($21,683 vs $34,710 median), suggesting the market is already pricing in automation vulnerability.</li>
    <li><strong>India faces a dual challenge of low pay and high vulnerability.</strong> Indian workers face comparable automation risk to the USA but earn 71% less in median salary, and their job survival scores are the lowest of any country (0.87 vs 1.52 in the USA).</li>
    <li><strong>Skill demand is shifting toward technical depth.</strong> Deep Learning, Python, and Security skills correlate with both higher survival and lower AI risk, while Excel and SQL-centric roles are declining in relative resilience.</li>
